<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bahey Tharwat
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Authors are with Mohamed bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence (MBZUAI), Abu Dhabi, UAE.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yara Nasser
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Author is with Alexandria University, Alexandria, Egypt.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ali Abouzeid
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Authors are with Mohamed bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence (MBZUAI), Abu Dhabi, UAE.
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ian Reid
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Authors are with Mohamed bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence (MBZUAI), Abu Dhabi, UAE.
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have gained popularity for learning robotic manipulation tasks that follow language instructions. State-of-the-art VLAs, such as OpenVLA and <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, were trained on large-scale, manually labeled action datasets collected through teleoperation. More recent approaches, including LAPA and villa-X, introduce latent action representations that enable unsupervised pretraining on unlabeled datasets by modeling abstract visual changes between frames. Although these methods have shown strong results, their large model sizes make deployment in real-world settings challenging.
In this work, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAWM</span>, a model-agnostic framework to pretrain imitation learning models in a self-supervised way, by learning latent action representations from unlabeled video data through world modeling. These videos can be sourced from robot recordings or videos of humans performing actions with everyday objects. Our framework is designed to be effective for transferring across tasks, environments, and embodiments. It outperforms models trained with ground-truth robotics actions and similar pretraining methods on the LIBERO benchmark and real-world setup, while being significantly more efficient and practical for real-world settings.</p>
  
</div>
<figure id="S0.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18428v1/framework2.png" id="S0.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/115;" width="452" height="115" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Overview of <span id="S0.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAWM</span>, which consists of two stages :
(a) <span id="S0.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent Action Pretraining</span>, where an imitation learning model processes input images and natural language instructions to predict latent action representations <math id="S0.F2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{t:t+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t:t+n}</annotation></semantics></math>. These are paired with video frames and optimized jointly with a world model in a self-supervised manner by predicting future frames, grounding the latent actions in environment dynamics without reliance on ground-truth actions.
(b) <span id="S0.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action Finetuning</span>, where the pretrained imitation learning model is adapted to downstream robotic tasks using labeled demonstrations. Here, the model maps observations (images, language instructions , and robot state) directly to ground-truth actions <math id="S0.F2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{t:t+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t:t+n}</annotation></semantics></math> through supervised learning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-supervised learning has been a key enabler of recent breakthroughs in Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and Gemini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, where models learn from large amounts of text on the Internet. Inspired by this success, the robotics community is now ready for its own transformative moment, where we can build systems that learn action representations directly from raw, unstructured video data, rather than relying on curated action labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most current approaches to robot learning are heavily based on supervised learning frameworks. Methods like imitation learning and VLA models, including OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, require paired image action datasets often obtained through teleoperation. These action annotations are expensive to collect, difficult to scale, and prone to bias, limiting the generalizability of these systems across tasks, environments, and embodiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAWM</span>, a <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>atent <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>ction pretraining framework through <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">W</span>orld <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M</span>odeling that aims to overcome these limitations by combining an Imitation Learning Model with a World Model. Our objective, as shown in Fig. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, is to learn action representations from both robot-collected and human demonstration videos in a fully self-supervised way. These learned representations serve as action priors that can be effectively leveraged during finetuning on downstream tasks. The proposed framework, illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, is designed to be <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model-agnostic</span>, meaning that it does not depend on any specific architecture for the imitation learning model or the world model. This flexibility allows for the integration of a variety of different models.
Our pipeline follows a two-stage steps. The first stage is an end-to-end pretraining in a self-supervised way, with the learning signal derived from predicting the next image in a video sequence. The inputs to the system consist of: (i) an image frame from a human or robot performing a manipulation task, and (ii) a natural language instruction describing the goal of the task. The imitation learning model takes these inputs to produce <span id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">action chunks representations</span>. These representations of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> actions are paired with the current image frame and the next <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> frames, then fed into the world model to generate the next image frames that would result from executing the action in the current environment. The second stage is a finetuning stage, where labeled data are used to finetune only the imitation learning model to downstream tasks. During this phase, the world model is no longer used. The learned imitation learning model is now equipped with a robust prior from large-scale unlabeled videos and can be finetuned efficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize our main contributions and findings below:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAWM</span>, a <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model-agnostic</span> framework, to learn action chunk representations for imitation learning models from both robot and human videos without action labels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments show that our framework can learn superior action priors from human demonstrations and robotic manipulation videos without using ground-truth action labels, compared to supervised pretraining.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that our framework with small models such as BAKU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and Dreamerv3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> outperforms similar methods with large models such as villa-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> on the LIBERO benchmark  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in Vision-Language Models (VLMs) trained on large-scale internet text, image, and video datasets have paved the way in Robotics for Vision-Language-Navigation (VLN) models  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and for Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. These models extend VLMs by grounding multimodal reasoning in robotic action spaces, enabling robots to follow natural language instructions and generalize to new tasks.
Datasets such as Open X-Embodiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> advances this direction by unifying multi-robot data, which supports large-scale models like OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Imitation Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Imitation learning approaches leverage demonstrations to directly map observations to actions, enabling robots to learn from expert behavior or learned policies. At the large-scale end of the spectrum, <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenVLA</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> is a 7B parameter VLA model with dual visual encoders (DINOv2  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and SigLIP  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>). It is trained on the Open X-Embodiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> dataset with 970k real robot demonstrations. Similarly, leveraging large-scale architectures, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> is a 3.3B parameter VLA model that leverages a pretrained VLM backbone to inherit Internet-scale semantic knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taking a contrasting lightweight approach, <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BAKU</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> is a compact 7M parameter model that fuses multimodal inputs: vision, proprioception, and task instructions through a FiLM-conditioned encoder  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and MLPs. Its observation trunk can be either an MLP or a causal transformer decoder to output action actions. BAKU was originally trained and evaluated on the LIBERO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> benchmark. Another line of work explores action generation through generative modeling. <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Policy</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> frames robot control as a conditional denoising diffusion process that refines noisy action sequences into smooth trajectories. Building on temporal reasoning, <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Action Chunking Transformer (ACT)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> tackles long-horizon planning by predicting ’chunks’ of future actions instead of single steps, reducing error accumulation and improving efficiency in long tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">World Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">World models learn representations of environment dynamics to support planning and control in embodied agents. <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DIAMOND</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> is a diffusion-based world model that takes sequences of past frames and actions to predict future frames via denoising diffusion, demonstrating strong performance in visually rich environments such as CSGO. In contrast, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dreamer</span> world models family  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> implements a more sophisticated approach through its <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM)</span> architecture, which encodes sensory inputs into compact stochastic representations and uses a recurrent sequence model to predict future states given past actions. This RSSM framework enables the agent to learn a comprehensive world model of its environment and leverage it to plan by <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">imagining</em> potential future trajectories, facilitating efficient planning and policy learning in high-dimensional control tasks. From the learned model state, DreamerV3 could predict rewards, continuation signals, and reconstruct observations to ensure informative latent representations, while offering flexible scalability with model sizes ranging from <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1 million to 200 million parameters</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Action Methods</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early works explored the pretraining of vision encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> on egocentric human
videos  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, retargeting human motions to robots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, or predicting actions from inverse dynamics models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Latent action models in simulation and video games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> showed that abstract action spaces improve generalization, but remained limited to non-robotic domains. Building on these foundations, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LAPA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> introduced a 7B-parameter Vision-Language-Action model that learns latent actions through a three-stage pipeline, enabling training from unlabeled videos. However, it suffers from underperformance on fine-grained manipulation tasks, inference latency, and high computational cost. More recently, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">villa-X</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> introduces a 3B-parameter framework that jointly models latent and robot actions through diffusion-based prediction and improves upon LAPA by integrating a proprioceptive forward-dynamics model (FDM) into its Latent Action Model (LAM). This auxiliary component predicts future robot joint angles and gripper states from latent tokens. villa-X demonstrated better performance than LAPA on the SIMPLER benchmark  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, though it still requires the latent action module during finetuning and remains computationally expensive.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18428v1/figures/datasets.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/292;" width="429" height="292" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Benchmarks used for pretraining and finetuning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LAWM consists of two stages. The first stage is Latent Action Pretraining and the second is Action Fine-tuning:</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Action Pretraining</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The pretraining stage, illustrated in Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, is designed to be <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">model-agnostic</span>, trained end-to-end in a self-supervised way. allowing flexible integration of different architectures for both imitation learning and world models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The inputs to the system are: (i) an image frame from a human or robot performing a manipulation task, and (ii) a natural language instruction specifying a task objective (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Pick <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\text{object}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">object</mtext><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\text{object}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> into sink”</span>). The imitation learning model processes these inputs to produce <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> latent actions</span> that encode semantic and temporal aspects of the intended behavior. These latent actions are not explicitly supervised with ground-truth actions but instead are paired with the current image frame at the current timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and the next <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> frames, then fed into the world model to generate the next image frames from timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+n</annotation></semantics></math>, and the learning signal comes from predicting the next image frames in the video sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our case, to learn the dynamics of the world, we use a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) architecture with an encoder, dynamics model, and decoder, following DreamerV3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. At each timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the model maintains a deterministic recurrent state <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> updated by a transition function <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and samples a stochastic latent variable <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from a posterior conditioned on the current observation <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Recurrent state:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{t}=f_{\phi}(\hat{s}_{t-1},a_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{t}=f_{\phi}(\hat{s}_{t-1},a_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Representation model:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{t}\sim q_{\phi}(z_{t}\mid h_{t},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{t}\sim q_{\phi}(z_{t}\mid h_{t},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Dynamics predictor:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{z}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{z}_{t}\mid h_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{z}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{z}_{t}\mid h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S3.E4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Decoder:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{x}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{x}_{t}\mid\hat{s}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{x}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{x}_{t}\mid\hat{s}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where the model state is <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{t}=(h_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{t}=(h_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The posterior <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and prior <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are modeled as categorical distributions. The imitation learning and world models <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> are trained by minimizing an objective consisting of a reconstruction loss of the mean squared error (MSE) and a KL divergence regularizer:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S3.E5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:38.3pt;vertical-align:-13.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(35.7pt,-4.4pt) scale(1.29852190066668,1.29852190066668) ;">
<p id="S3.E5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.E5.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{q_{\phi}}\Bigg[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\underbrace{\|x_{t}-\hat{x}_{t}\|^{2}}_{\text{MSE Reconstruction}}+\beta\,\underbrace{\text{KL}\big(q_{\phi}(z_{t}\mid h_{t},x_{t})\,\|\,p_{\phi}(z_{t}\mid h_{t})\big)}_{\text{KL Regularization}}\Bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>MSE Reconstruction</mtext></munder></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mtext>KL</mtext><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>q</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>KL Regularization</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)=\mathbb{E}_{q_{\phi}}\Bigg[\sum_{t=1}^{T}\underbrace{\|x_{t}-\hat{x}_{t}\|^{2}}_{\text{MSE Reconstruction}}+\beta\,\underbrace{\text{KL}\big(q_{\phi}(z_{t}\mid h_{t},x_{t})\,\|\,p_{\phi}(z_{t}\mid h_{t})\big)}_{\text{KL Regularization}}\Bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of KL regularization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">This enables the framework to jointly optimize the imitation learning and world model together in an end-to-end way to produce action representations that are grounded in the environment dynamics, effectively capturing a policy without reliance on ground-truth action supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Action Finetuning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the self-supervised pretraining stage, we then proceed to a finetuning stage where the training signal is derived from supervised learning with ground-truth actions. In this stage, the world model is no longer used. Instead, the pretrained imitation learning model (in our case, BAKU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> or Diffusion Policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) is trained on specific downstream tasks to adapt it to the target environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The pretrained model benefits from a robust prior obtained in the pretraining stage from large-scale unlabeled video data, which enables efficient finetuning on labeled datasets. Finetuning can be performed either by (i) adding an additional head that decodes the latent action representation into the ground-truth action space, or (ii) reinitializing the final action latent layer to match the dimensionality of the ground-truth action space and training it accordingly. In our implementation, we adopt the second strategy, reinitializing the last action latent layer during finetuning as LAPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> demonstrated that this works better empirically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For BAKU, the loss objective is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{BAKU}}=-\;\mathbb{E}_{(o_{t},a_{t})\sim\mathcal{D}^{e}}\Bigl[\;\log\pi^{IL}(a_{t}\mid o_{t})\;\Bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>BAKU</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>e</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.280em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{BAKU}}=-\;\mathbb{E}_{(o_{t},a_{t})\sim\mathcal{D}^{e}}\Bigl[\;\log\pi^{IL}(a_{t}\mid o_{t})\;\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expert observations at time <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., multi-view camera inputs, proprioceptive states, and task text instruction),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the expert action at time <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{IL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{IL}</annotation></semantics></math> is the imitation learning policy, modeled as a conditional distribution over actions,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>e</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{e}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dataset of expert demonstrations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This corresponds to minimizing the negative log-likelihood (NLL) of expert actions under the learned policy distribution, i.e., a maximum likelihood estimation objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For Diffusion Policy, the loss objective is expressed as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{Diffusion}}=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon_{k},a_{t},o_{t},k}\Big[\;\|\epsilon_{k}-\epsilon_{\theta}(o_{t},a_{t}+\epsilon_{k},k)\|^{2}\;\Big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>Diffusion</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.280em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{Diffusion}}=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon_{k},a_{t},o_{t},k}\Big[\;\|\epsilon_{k}-\epsilon_{\theta}(o_{t},a_{t}+\epsilon_{k},k)\|^{2}\;\Big]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where:</p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> : denotes the expert observations at time <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., multi-view camera inputs, proprioceptive states, and task text instruction),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> : denotes the expert action at time <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> : Gaussian noise at diffusion step <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> : the diffusion timestep index,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(o_{t},a_{t}+\epsilon_{k},k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(o_{t},a_{t}+\epsilon_{k},k)</annotation></semantics></math> : the denoising network prediction of the noise, conditioned on the observation <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the noised action <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}+\epsilon_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}+\epsilon_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and timestep <math id="S3.I2.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This objective trains the diffusion model to correctly predict the added noise <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> given the noised action and the current observation, effectively learning the conditional distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(a_{t}\mid o_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(a_{t}\mid o_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> over actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Through this stage, the imitation learning model learns to map image-text inputs to explicit actions, aligning its predictions with supervised ground-truth demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.18428v1/figures/results/single_bar_chart_custom_narrows_baku-min.png" id="S3.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/188;" width="215" height="188" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Success rates of <span id="S3.F4.sf1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BAKU</span> on LIBERO-90 under different pretraining strategies.
WM = world modeling without action labels.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.18428v1/figures/results/single_bar_chart_custom_narrows_dp-min.png" id="S3.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/188;" width="215" height="188" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Success rates of <span id="S3.F4.sf2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Policy</span> on LIBERO-90 under different pretraining strategies.
WM = world modeling without action labels.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Comparison of success rates under different pretraining strategies for (a) BAKU and (b) Diffusion Policy on LIBERO-90 benchmark.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on answering the following research questions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1.</span> Can LAWM learn superior priors compared to those learned using ground-truth actions during pretraining?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2.</span> How does LAWM perform compared to recent similar methods like villa-X?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3.</span> Is it feasible to pretrain and learn robust action priors solely from raw human manipulation videos?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q4.</span> Do the learned latent actions correlate with ground-truth actions?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Benchmarks and Environments</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate LAWM across diverse datasets and environments that enable both large-scale pretraining and downstream finetuning.
An overview of the benchmarks used in our work is shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ II-C Latent Action Methods ‣ II Related Work ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BridgeData v2</span> (BDv2)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>: A large-scale dataset comprising 60,096 trajectories collected across 24 environments using a low-cost robot. It supports multi-task and multi-environment learning, featuring diverse manipulation tasks such as pick-and-place, pushing, and folding, with over 100 objects involved.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Something-Something v2</span> (SSv2)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>: A video dataset containing 220,847 labeled video clips of humans performing actions with everyday objects. Although not a robotic dataset, it is widely used for training models in multi-modal and temporal understanding tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LIBERO Benchmark</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>: The LIBERO benchmark contains LIBERO-90, which are 90 tasks spanning diverse objects, layouts, and goals, and is often used as the default evaluation benchmark in many works. LIBERO also has four task suites: LIBERO-Spatial evaluates the model performance in novel layouts with the same tasks and object types, LIBERO-Object evaluates the model performance with novel object types with the same tasks and layouts, LIBERO-Goal evaluates the model performance under novel tasks with the same types and layouts of objects, and LIBERO-Long evaluates the model’s performance under a diverse set of objects, layouts, and backgrounds, focusing on long-horizon tasks. Each task suite contains 10 tasks with 50 demonstrations per task for finetuning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world Setup</span>: We further validate our approach on a real-world tabletop manipulation setup using a 6-DoF Realman robot arm equipped with a 1-DoF gripper. We collect a custom dataset comprising five tasks: three pick-and-place tasks, one stacking task, and one object-moving task. Each scene was captured from two fixed, distinct camera viewpoints to provide multi-view observations. For each task, we recorded 50 demonstrations using human teleoperation with a Meta Quest VR controller. The task instructions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pick-and-Place:</span> (i) Put both cups on the plate, (ii) Pick the blue cup and place it in the bowl which is on top of the plate, (iii) Pick the pineapple out of the pot and place it in between the cups; <span id="S4.I2.i4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Move:</span> Move the pineapple to the pot and the roll to the plate; <span id="S4.I2.i4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stack:</span> Stack the blue cup on top of the white one.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18428v1/realman_test.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/138;" width="476" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Qualitative results of our framework on the real-world manipulation setup using the 6-DoF Realman robot arm. Top row: Picking the blue cup and placing it in the bowl on top of the plate. Bottom row: Stacking the blue cup on top of the white cup. Images are shown across consecutive timesteps.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baselines and Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate LAWM with two strong imitation learning baselines: BAKU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and Diffusion Policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
To address our research questions (<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1–Q4</span>), we design the following experiments (<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E1–E4</span>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E1. Superior priors vs. ground-truth pretraining.</span>
We establish two performance bounds on the LIBERO-90 benchmark. First, the baseline models are trained from scratch on LIBERO-90. Second, baseline models are pretrained on the ground-truth actions from BridgeData v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, then finetuned on LIBERO-90. To evaluate our approach relative to these bounds, we pretrain our framework on each of Something-Something v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and BridgeData v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> without action labels, followed by finetuning on LIBERO-90.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E2. Comparison with similar methods.</span>
We pretrain on a mixture of BridgeData v2 and Something-Something v2 datasets, then finetune on the LIBERO task suites (Spatial, Object, Goal, and Long), same as villa-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, to enable direct comparison. We also compare with other state-of-the-art models pretrained with supervised learning on ground-truth actions and VLAs that are initialized from VLMs trained on internet scale data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E3. Pretraining solely from human videos.</span>
We experiment learning action priors from raw human manipulation videos by pretraining only on the Something-Something v2 dataset. We also study how model size affects the quality of the learned priors and the performance when finetuned on the challenging suite LIBERO-Long.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">E4. Correlation of latent and ground-truth actions.</span>
To quantify the alignment between our learned latent actions and ground-truth actions, we use Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA). CCA finds linear transformations of two sets of variables such that their transformed representations are maximally correlated. Formally, given latent actions <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}</annotation></semantics></math> and ground-truth actions <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times q}</annotation></semantics></math>, CCA finds weight vectors <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> that maximize the correlation between transformed variables <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=Za,\quad v=Yb," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=Za,\quad v=Yb,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">CCA produces pairs of canonical components <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{i},v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{i},v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> that capture the linear correlations between latent and ground-truth actions, ordered from strongest to weakest. The first pair <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{1},v_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{1},v_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, known as the first <em id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">canonical components</em> has the strongest correlation. The correlation factor is simply the <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pearson correlation coefficient</span> between transformed variables <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{\operatorname{cov}(u,v)}{\sqrt{\operatorname{var}(u)}\,\sqrt{\operatorname{var}(v)}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{\operatorname{cov}(u,v)}{\sqrt{\operatorname{var}(u)}\,\sqrt{\operatorname{var}(v)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{cov}(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{cov}(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the covariance between <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{var}(u),\operatorname{var}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{var}(u),\operatorname{var}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> are their variances.</p>
</div>
