</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were conducted on a single A100 GPU with default settings, unless otherwise specified: a DreamerV3 world model with 50M parameters configuration and a latent action space of dimension 7, same dimension as the ground-truth actions. The action chunk size is set to 10 future actions for BAKU experiments and 16 for Diffusion Policy experiments. We only use 10% of the Something-Something v2 dataset for pretraining. With these settings, pretraining on each of the datasets BridgeData v2 and Something-Something v2 requires approximately 30 hours, while finetuning on LIBERO-90 takes around 24 hours, and fine-tuning on the LIBERO suites (Spatial, Object, Goal, Long) takes approximately 2 hours each. Finetuning on our custom real-world dataset takes only about 20 minutes. The evaluation metric used is the Success Rate (SR), defined as the percentage of successful trials out of the total attempts. We report SR over 10 evaluation trials per each task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results &amp; Discussion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present quantitative and qualitative results, and discuss our findings of our framework across the LIBERO benchmark, its task suites, and a real-world setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R1. Superior priors vs. ground-truth pretraining.</span>
We use BAKU and Diffusion Policy on LIBERO-90 tasks as baselines. An interesting key finding is that pretraining on robotics or human video datasets <em id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> ground-truth actions, when combined with world models, can outperform supervised pretraining on action labels. We hypothesize that supervised pretraining on actions forces the model to map observations to actions, which can cause it to overfit to the distribution of BridgeData v2 actions (specific robots, trajectories, noise, task setups). However, world model pretraining encourage the model to learn state transitions and dynamics. This generalizes better when finetuned on downstream tasks, since the dynamics are more universal across tasks than the specific action distributions in BridgeData v2.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Results on LIBERO-90</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure  <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B Action Finetuning ‣ III Method ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Training from scratch provides baselines of 91.4% (BAKU) and 90.5% (DP), while supervised pretraining on BridgeData v2 improves performance to 93.3% and 92.1%, respectively.
World modeling pretraining results in more improvement: BAKU reaches 94.2% with BridgeData v2 (WM) and 92.6% with Something-Something v2 (WM), while Diffusion Policy achieves 93.7% and 92.6% under the same settings.
These results highlight that world modeling not only reduces reliance on action labels but can also match or surpass supervised pretraining across different models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Results on Real-world setup</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pretraining our framework solely on human videos from the Something-Something v2 dataset significantly improves performance on real-world tasks, achieving near-perfect success rates across five custom downstream tasks. Training only on our custom dataset without pretraining gives an average success rate of 84%, which increases to 94% with our pretraining framework. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ Results on Real-world setup ‣ V Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, these results show that world modeling improves performance not just in simulation but also in real-world tasks. The qualitative results of our framework on real-world testing are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-A Benchmarks and Environments ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, demonstrating the ability of our approach to handle complex real-world scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Success Rates (%) on Five Real Tasks</figcaption>
