<td id="S5.T2.1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">90.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">100.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.1.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">90.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.1.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">90.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.1.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">100.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.1.1.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">94.0</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R2. Comparison with similar methods.</span>
Since we validated our framework with baseline models in the previous section, we compare our framework using the BAKU model with other state-of-the-art models on the LIBERO task suites (Spatial, Object, Goal, Long). Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-B Baselines and Experimental Setup ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> compares three categories of models:
1) Models pretrained on ground-truth robotics actions (Octo-base <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>). 2) Models initialized from VLMs trained on internet scale data (<math id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> initialized from PaliGemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> and smolVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> from smolVLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>).
3) Models with similar objective as ours, relying on human and robot videos (villa-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>). villa-X has two variants: one removes the latent action module during finetuning, and another keeps it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first reproduce the BAKU results on the LIBERO task suites without any pretraining. BAKU provides a strong baseline, outperforming villa-x in the Object, Goal, and Long suites, while villa-X performs better in the Spatial suite. BAKU also outperforms other state-of-the-art methods and its performance is competitive with <math id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>, though <math id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math> remains superior overall. This is due to BAKU’s effective and efficient design for multi-task learning, despite its small size. With our proposed pretraining framework, we further improve upon BAKU, achieving the best overall success rate in most of the task suites, outperforming <math id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math> and others.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R3. Pretraining solely from human videos.</span>
Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-B Action Finetuning ‣ III Method ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that pretraining solely on human videos (Something-Something v2 dataset) can be close to or even surpass supervised pretraining on ground-truth actions from robotics dataset (BridgeData v2). After finetuning on the LIBERO-90 benchmark, BAKU achieved 93.3% with supervised pretraining and 92.6% with human videos pretraining, showing performance close to supervised pretraining. On the other hand, for the diffusion policy, the supervised pretraining reached 92. 1%, while the human videos pretraining reached 92. 6%, slightly surpassing the supervised baseline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also show in Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ Results on Real-world setup ‣ V Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> that scaling the world model during pretraining on Something-Something v2 alone improves performance when finetuned on LIBERO-Long. Although improvement plateaus at 100M parameters, we expect further gains could be achieved by adding more video data, in line with observed scaling laws <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18428v1/figures/world_model_ablation.png" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/253;" width="405" height="253" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>World model size in pretraining vs Success rate in finetuning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R4. Correlation of latent and ground-truth actions.</span>
We group actions by task type to assess correlation patterns across different manipulation tasks. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ Results on Real-world setup ‣ V Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, the first canonical component achieves strong correlations in all task categories: Put (0.9154), Move (0.9599), Remove (0.9098), and Take (0.9541). Our method shows better correlation factors than villa-X <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> in most tasks, which we attribute to the fact that our latent actions are produced by an imitation learning model conditioned on task instruction, aligning more closely with ground-truth actions. In contrast, villa-X relies on inverse dynamics models, whose latents primarily capture scene transitions rather than actions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSSx2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We further perform a qualitative analysis of the learned latent actions by visualizing image pairs that share similar action latent representations. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Fig. 7 ‣ Results on Real-world setup ‣ V Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Latent Action Pretraining Through World Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, these pairs correspond to similar underlying robot behaviors, highlighting the meaningful features captured by our latent space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE III: </span>CCA First Coefficient per Task</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:267.7pt;height:35.3pt;vertical-align:-15.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-7.0pt,0.9pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
